 {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "27 April 1906"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Intercalated"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/214"} "Ormos Falirou, Peiraias"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/157533"} "2 from 1 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "1,000 metres. Final only."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The single sculls event was held over 1000-meters. It is listed in the official programme as “Course de Canoë.” However, this only appears to be a Greek difficulty translating French in the official results and the programme. There were only two rowers in this event, both from France, and the winner was " [:a {:href "/athletes/14121"} "Gaston Delaplane"] ". Pictures in the French sporting magazine " [:em {} "La Vie Au Grand Air"] " show pictures of Delaplane in a standard scull. Interestingly, in the official entry lists, there are no entries listed for the singles event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The top rowers in this era were considered to be the British, and possibly the Canadians. Britain’s top sculler was " [:a {:href "/athletes/37350"} "Frederick Kelly"] ", who had won the Diamond Challenge Sculls at Henley in 1902-03 and 1905. In 1904, Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/36263"} "Lou Scholes"] " had defeated Kelly for that title, and he had won several Canadian and American championships."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/14121"} "Gaston Delaplane"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "5:53.4"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/79894"} "Joseph Larran"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "6:07.2"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]]]] "\n"] "\n" [:footer {:class "footer"} "\n" [:br {}] "\n" [:div {:class "panel panel-info"} "\n" [:div {:class "panel-heading"} "\n" [:h3 {:class "panel-title"} "\n" [:span {:class "glyphicon glyphicon-info-sign"}] "\nDid you know?\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "panel-body"} "\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/99870"} "Simon Ammann"] " won ski jumping gold medals on the Normal Hill and Large Hill at the 2002 Salt Lake City Olympics. His victories were a huge upset, as he placed only 17th overall in the World Cup that season."] "\n\n\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:p {} "\n" [:a {:href "/users/sign_in"} "Login"] "\n|\n" [:a {:href "/statistics/site"} "Statistics"] "\n|\n" [:a {:href "/static/about"} "About"] "\n|\n" [:a {:href "/static/faq"} "FAQ"] "\n|\n" [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"] "\n"] "\n" [:p {} "\n© " [:a {:href "/static/about"} "OlyMADMen"] " 2006 — 2026\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n" [:script {:src "/assets/lightbox.min-a06f7b5a37bdc88a78a48f95b9149ca917761a07bffb855a6d73c1a435e16552.js"}] #object[org.jsoup.nodes.TextNode 0x206d4420 ""] [:script {} "\n  $('.footnote').tooltip();\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n")